# Survival of Battleship 

Hey there! Welcome to Survival of Battleship, a fully interactive 3D naval survival game built from scratch using Python and PyOpenGL. 

In this game, you take full command of a battleship navigating through open waters. It’s not just about sailing around, though - you’ll have to survive brutal seasonal rainstorms, battle aggressive enemy ships that try to sink you, and accurately steer your way through terrifying, massive rogue waves.

# Core Gameplay & Cool Features

*Dynamic Ocean Grid: The water isn't just a flat plane. It uses sine and cosine math loops to create shifting wave colors and realistic white foam streaks that zoom past your ship as you pick up speed.
*Active Weather System: Watch the sky transition through a continuous day and night cycle. Be prepared for sudden, heavy rainstorms that can roll in at any moment.
*The Rogue Wave Threat: Right after a storm clears up, a giant rogue wave will spawn and rush toward you. If you don't face it head on, your hull will take massive damage.
*Tactical AI Enemies: Enemy ships will spawn, track your location, and try to maintain a perfect distance to orbit you while firing synchronized, explosive broadside cannon volleys.
*Dual Camera Views: You can easily switch between a cinematic 3rd-person tactical view or jump right down onto the deck in a 1st-person navigation view.


# Game Controls Reference

# Controlling Your Sails & Steering
* 'W' : Raise Sail / Speed Up (No Sail ➔ Half Sail ➔ Full Sail)
* 'S' : Lower Sail / Slow Down (Full Sail ➔ Half Sail ➔ No Sail)
* 'A' : Steer Port (Left) — *Note: Your rudder only works if the ship is actually moving!*
* 'D' : Steer Starboard (Right) — *Note: Your rudder only works if the ship is actually moving!*

#Combat & Aiming
Your cannons are permanently mounted to the sides of your ship, so positioning matters!
* 'Q' : Hold down to project the Left (Port) aiming line.
* 'E' : Hold down to project the Right (Starboard) aiming line.
* 'J' : Fire Left Cannons.
* 'L' : Fire Right Cannons.

# Camera Adjustments
* 'V' : Toggle View (Switch between 3rd-person and 1st-person deck view).
* 'Up Arrow' : Zoom In (3rd Person) / Move Camera Higher (1st Person).
* ' Down Arrow' : Zoom Out (3rd Person) / Move Camera Lower (1st Person).
* 'Left Arrow' : Rotate camera angle to the left.
* 'Right Arrow' : Rotate camera angle to the right.

# Handy System Keys
* 'C' : Toggle Autopilot / Cheat Mode (Turns on an automatic captain that steers and shoots enemies for you).
* 'N' : Instantly switch between Day and Night.
* 'T' : Turn the rain visual overlay on or off.
* 'R' : Reset / Restart Game (Can be used anytime, especially if you sink!).
* 'ESC' : Safely close the game window.


# Quick Tactical Tips to Survive Longer

1.  Don't Rush the Storms: Keeping your ship at *Full Sail* during an active storm will batter your hull and cause constant structural damage. Lower your sails to *Half Sail* or drop them entirely to ride out the storm safely.
2.  Surviving the Rogue Wave: When a storm ends, keep your eyes peeled for the giant rogue wave. You must turn your ship's bow directly into the wave. If you take the wave from the side (anything over a 35-degree angle), it will deal devastating damage to your health bar.
3.  Perfect Your Broadsides: Don't sail directly at enemy ships. Since you can only shoot from your left and right flanks, you want to sail parallel to your targets, line up your green aiming lasers, and unleash your cannons!

# Getting Started

# 1. Prerequisites
To run this game, you'll need Python 3 installed on your computer, along with the PyOpenGL package. You can install the required libraries by running this quick command in your terminal or command prompt:

'bash
pip install PyOpenGL PyOpenGL_accelerate
'

# 2. How to Run the Game

1. Download or clone this repository to your local machine.
2. Open your terminal, navigate to the folder where the files are located, and run:

'bash
python Battle_ShipUP.py
'

# Built With

* Python 3 - Core programming language.
* PyOpenGL - Python bindings for OpenGL graphics rendering.
* GLUT & GLU - Used for managing the window, capturing keyboard inputs, and projecting the 3D camera perspective.